{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"birding-big-year-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Birding big year track","description":"Plans and runs a birding big year in gated steps from rules and area through a target list and seasonal calendar to trip plans and monthly reviews, with ethics and budget kept in check.","category":"pastimes","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","operate","review"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences","notes"],"output":["plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["big-year","year-list","bird-listing","bird-migration","birding-trips","field-skills"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-birdwatching-outing","identify-bird-from-description"]},"args":[{"name":"area","description":"The area your year list covers, for example \"my county\", \"a 5 km circle from home\", \"the whole of Portugal\".","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"year","description":"The year or 12-month period, for example \"2027\" or \"April 2027 to March 2028\".","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"constraints","description":"Time, budget and travel limits, for example \"weekends only, 1,000 EUR, no flights\", \"car-free, cycling only\".","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"experience","description":"Your birding level.","type":"enum","enum":["beginner","intermediate","expert"],"default":"intermediate"}],"steps":[{"id":"rules","file":"steps/01-rules.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"targets","file":"steps/02-targets.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"calendar","file":"steps/03-calendar.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"trip","file":"steps/04-trip.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"review","file":"steps/05-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs a birding big year in {{area}} over {{year}} within {{constraints}}, pitched at a {{experience}} birder. It fixes the rules first, builds a realistic target list and a seasonal calendar, then plans trips and reviews progress each month so the plan adapts to what has been seen and what is left. It never invents species occurrence, dates or sites for the area: it reasons from the birder's own knowledge, regional guides and local records, and marks every timing as \"to check\". Ethics come before the list: no disturbance of nesting or roosting birds, no playback where it harms birds or is banned, and no trespass. Steps 4 and 5 repeat through the year. If the birder asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining planning steps in one reply and state each choice made at a skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"rules","file":"steps/01-rules.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Rules and goal\n\nFix the rules of the year before any planning.\n\nArea: {{area}}\nPeriod: {{year}}\nConstraints: {{constraints}}\nExperience: {{experience}}\n\n1. If the area boundary is unclear (a county name used in several countries, a radius without a centre), ask and stop.\n2. Propose the rules: exact boundary, start and end dates, which species count (wild, established introductions per the regional list authority, heard-only allowed or not), whether rarities need acceptance by the local records committee, and whether other people's sightings count only if you see the bird yourself.\n3. Set the style: a green or low-carbon year, a patch year, or a car-based year, matched to {{constraints}}.\n4. Set a goal as a range, explaining how to estimate it from the area's typical annual species total in recent local reports (to look up) and the time available. Do not invent a number for the area.\n5. List the ethics code you will keep.\n\nStop and wait for the birder to approve or change the rules and goal."},{"id":"targets","file":"steps/02-targets.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Target list\n\nBuild the list of species to plan around.\n\n1. Ask the birder to paste or describe the area's species list from a regional checklist or recent annual report, or to name the groups they know are present. Work from that; do not invent species for the area.\n2. Sort the species into tiers: common residents that will come with normal birding, seasonal visitors (summer, winter, passage), scarce regulars that need a dedicated trip, and rarities that cannot be planned.\n3. For each tier, note the habitat and season, and flag the hard identification groups for a {{experience}} birder (for example gulls, warblers, waders, raptors) with what to study first.\n4. Highlight the species most likely to be missed if not targeted at the right time, such as short passage windows or nocturnal birds.\n\nPresent a table: Species or group | Tier | Habitat | Best months (to check) | Notes. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"calendar","file":"steps/03-calendar.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Seasonal calendar\n\nTurn the targets into a 12-month plan within {{constraints}}.\n\n1. Lay out month by month: which tiers and species to prioritise, which habitats to visit, and key events (dawn chorus, spring and autumn passage, winter roosts, seabird season), with timings marked \"to check\" against local records.\n2. Spread effort so the January surge, spring passage and autumn passage get enough time, and quiet months are used for scarce residents and skills study.\n3. Fit trips to the budget and travel rules, and note what to drop if time runs short.\n4. Add a weekly rhythm: a regular patch visit, checking local bird news, and logging.\n\nPresent a month table: Month | Priorities | Places or habitats | Effort. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"trip","file":"steps/04-trip.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Trip plan\n\nPlan the next outing or trip. Repeat this step as often as needed.\n\n1. Ask for the date, the place or habitat, the target species still needed and the time available.\n2. Plan the timing (dawn or dusk, tide state for waders, weather that brings migrants), a route, and the order of sites.\n3. For each target, the key features and calls to check, the confusion species, and how to confirm the identification.\n4. Ethics and access for the sites: stay on paths, keep distance from nests and roosts, check access rules, and no playback where harmful or banned.\n5. Kit and logistics, and what to record: species, numbers, time, place and any photos or notes for rarities.\n\nStop and wait for the birder to approve or adjust before the trip."},{"id":"review","file":"steps/05-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Monthly review\n\nReview progress and adjust the plan.\n\n1. Ask for the current list, species added this month, and time and money spent.\n2. Compare with the calendar: what is on track, what was missed and whether it can still be found later in the year.\n3. Re-rank the remaining targets by chance and effort, and update the next month's plan.\n4. Check the budget and the birder's energy; suggest cuts before burnout.\n5. Note any records that need submitting to the local recorder or records committee.\n\nPresent: Progress summary, Missed and still possible, Next month's priorities, Records to submit. Return to step 4 for the next trip."}]}
